---
import DateExplorer from './DateExplorer.astro'
import CalendarSystemComparison from './CalendarSystemComparison.astro'
---

<nav class="home-paths not-content" aria-label="Explore Taqwim">
  <a href="/guide/getting-started/">
    <span>Build your first calendar</span>
    <span>
      Installation and a working example <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
    </span>
  </a>
  <a href="/guide/theming/">
    <span>Make it yours</span>
    <span>
      33 themes and a shared token contract <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
    </span>
  </a>
  <a href="/guide/accessibility/">
    <span>Explore the keyboard</span>
    <span>
      Focus, navigation, selection, and RTL <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
    </span>
  </a>
</nav>

<section class="home-section home-date-section not-content" aria-labelledby="dates-heading">
  <div class="section-introduction">
    <h2 id="dates-heading">Start with a date.</h2>
    <p>Conversions and arithmetic work on their own. No framework, no DOM, and no calendar component required.</p>
    <p>Try the same Gregorian date with different systems. The rules you choose determine the result.</p>
    <a href="/guide/dates/">
      Work with date utilities <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
  </div>
  <DateExplorer />
</section>

<section class="home-section not-content" aria-labelledby="systems-heading">
  <div class="section-introduction section-introduction-wide">
    <h2 id="systems-heading">The calendar system matters.</h2>
    <p>
      Umm al-Qura is the default: a deterministic table covering 1343–1500 AH. Islamic Civil and Islamic TBLA use
      explicit arithmetic rules. The same Hijri fields can represent different absolute days.
    </p>
    <a href="/guide/calendar-systems/">
      Understand the three systems <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
  </div>
  <CalendarSystemComparison />
</section>

<section class="home-section home-foundation not-content" aria-labelledby="foundation-heading">
  <div class="section-introduction">
    <h2 id="foundation-heading">
      One foundation.
      <br />
      Your framework.
    </h2>
    <p>
      Grid building, selection, paging, and keyboard navigation live in one shared state machine. Each adapter binds
      that behaviour to its framework.
    </p>
    <a href="/reference/options/">
      Explore the calendar options <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
  </div>
  <dl class="foundation-options">
    <div>
      <dt>Headless</dt>
      <dd>Behaviour and accessible attributes. Bring your own markup and styles.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Styled</dt>
      <dd>Ready-to-use calendars and date pickers, built from the public headless components.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Shared themes</dt>
      <dd>One CSS custom-property contract across frameworks. Choose a theme with an attribute.</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>

<footer class="home-footer not-content">
  <div>
    <strong>
      Taqwim{' '}
      <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">
        تقويم
      </span>
    </strong>
    <p>Careful dates. Considered interfaces.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    Inspired by <a href="https://github.com/acamarata/luxon-hijri">luxon-hijri</a> by Aric Camarata. Open source under
    the MIT license.
  </p>
</footer>
